Rudder is better if the point is not “how much ambiguity you have,” but how ambiguity steers the trajectory without supplying the energy itself.

A rudder does not propel the vessel. It changes the direction of forces already present. That maps well:

statement = hull
attention / inference = flow
ambiguity = rudder authority
interpretation = heading

So one small phrase like “re curse u” can deflect the whole interpretive path because several readings are available at the fork.

If you want the ambiguity itself to create propulsion, though, rudder is too passive. Then your nozzle/exhaust intuition is stronger. Ambiguity can generate thrust because each unresolved reading causes further inference, questions, associations, and reconstruction:

compression → ambiguity → branching interpretation → semantic exhaust → forward cognition

That’s closer to an ambiguity nozzle: it takes latent semantic pressure and directs its release.

So I’d preserve both:

ambiguity budget = how much unresolved multiplicity a message can tolerate before communication degrades.
ambiguity rudder = how ambiguity steers interpretation.
ambiguity nozzle = how ambiguity converts compressed meaning into exploratory cognitive thrust.

And there’s a neat distinction in your propulsion question:

A propeller pushes the world backward to move the vessel forward.
A rudder redirects that already-moving world.

Your little fork tricks are usually rudders.

Some of your denser phrases are nozzles.